use std
into garment.amni

Imports: math and gfx.cloth; namespace garment. This module has no main and does not depend on the renderer. It cuts a sweater out of a body's fitting shell (a skin-tight mesh around the body, such as MakeHuman's `helper-tights` group), loosens it off the body, adds rib bands at the hem and the cuffs and a neck band, gives it texture coordinates in meters for the knit and weave patterns, and drapes it with `gfx.cloth`. Positions are in the shell's own units with y up and the body facing +z; a spec's `unit` is meters per shell unit. All numbers are `real` unless a block says otherwise. Calls into `cloth` use names that already exist there (`cloth::make`, `cloth::pin`, `cloth::skin`, `cloth::step`, `cloth::particle`, `cloth::pos`, `cloth::cloths`); never define them here.

`Spec` is a struct of real fields with these defaults: `unit` 0.1 (meters per shell unit; MakeHuman meshes are in decimeters), `ease` 0.025 (meters of room between the shell and the main fabric), `rib_ease` 0.004 (the same for the rib bands), `hem` -0.03 (the hem's height in meters above the pelvis joint; negative is below it), `sleeve` 1.0 (the share of the arm from the shoulder joint to the wrist joint that the sleeve covers), `band` 0.06, `cuff` 0.07 and `collar` 0.02 (heights in meters of the hem band, the cuffs and the neck band; with a collar of 0 there is no neck band), `neck` 0.075 (the neck opening's radius in meters), `drop` 0.07 (meters below the neck joint where the neck opening stops; MakeHuman's neck joint is mid-neck), `scoop` 0.035 (meters it sits lower again at the front), `blouse` 0.05 (meters beyond a band's edge over which the ease grows from `rib_ease` to the full ease), `slack` 0.04 and `sleeve_slack` 0.05 (meters of extra length that the drape gathers above the hem band and along each sleeve), `gap` 0.002 (meters the draped fabric keeps from the shell), `fine` 1.0 (how many times the garment's triangles are split into four before the drape). `fn spec() Spec` returns the defaults.

Results, module vars that `make` rebuilds from empty: `pos` and `nrm` (f32, three per vertex), `tan` (f32, four per vertex: a glTF tangent, the unit direction of increasing u along the surface and then the sign w, so that the cross product of the normal and the tangent times w points toward decreasing v), `uv` (f32, two per vertex, in meters: u runs around the body or the arm and v along it, so knit columns run down the body and along the arms), `body` and `rib` (u32, three vertex numbers per triangle: the main fabric and the rib bands), `part` (int per vertex: 0 torso, 1 left arm, 2 right arm, 3 neck band), `anchor` and `anchor_n` (f32, three per vertex: the shell point the vertex grew from and the shell's outward normal there), `held` (bool per vertex: the drape holds it) and `goal` (f32, three per vertex: where the drape holds a held vertex). Working state, also module vars rebuilt by `make`: `S` (the Spec in use), `jt` (real, the 30 joint numbers), `sn` (real, three per shell vertex: its normal), `sp` (int per shell vertex: its part), `st` (real per shell vertex: its place along its part in meters), and the work mesh: `wp`, `wa`, `wn` (real, three per work vertex: position, anchor point, anchor normal), `wq` (int: part), `wt` (real: place along the part in meters, as for `st`), `wh` (bool: held), `wg` (real, three: goal), `wi` (int, three per work triangle), `wr` (bool per work triangle: it belongs to a rib band) and `wb` (int, two work vertex numbers per boundary edge, in the order its triangle lists them).

`joint(k int) real, real, real` returns joint k's x, y, z from `jt`. The joints, in order: 0 pelvis, 1 spine, 2 chest, 3 neck, 4 left shoulder, 5 left elbow, 6 left wrist, 7 right shoulder, 8 right elbow, 9 right wrist. The torso polyline runs through joints 0, 1, 2, 3; the left arm polyline through 4, 5, 6 and the right arm polyline through 7, 8, 9. `arm_len(side int) real` is the length in meters of arm side's polyline (side 1 left, 2 right): the two segment lengths summed, times `S.unit`.

`on_part(q int, x real, y real, z real) real, real, real, real, real, real, real` finds the point of part q's polyline nearest to (x, y, z) and returns: the nearest point's x, y, z; the unit direction x, y, z of the segment it lies on; and the place along the part in meters. For the torso (q 0 or 3) every segment is clamped to its ends and the place is y times `S.unit`. For an arm the first segment is clamped to its ends but the last segment is a ray that goes on past the wrist (the projection parameter is only clamped below at 0), and the place is the distance along the polyline from the shoulder to the nearest point, times `S.unit`. A segment shorter than 1e-9 is skipped.

`classify(P, I)` (P: read-only f32 slice of shell positions, three per vertex; I: read-only int slice, three shell vertex numbers per triangle) fills `sn`, `sp` and `st`. Shell normals: for each triangle add the cross product of its edges (b minus a) and (c minus a) to its three corners, then scale each vertex's sum to length one (leave (0, 1, 0) when its length is below 1e-20). Part: for each shell vertex call `on_part` for parts 0, 1 and 2, take its distance to each nearest point minus the part's radius over `S.unit` (0.12 for the torso, 0.045 for the arms), and pick the part with the smallest value; `st` is that part's place.

`neck_m(x real, y real, z real) real` is how far a point lies outside the neck opening, in meters (negative inside it): with dx and dz the offset of x and z from the neck joint's x and z, d the length of (dx, dz) and front equal to dz over d (0 when d is below 1e-9), it is the larger of (d times `S.unit` minus (`S.neck` plus `S.scoop` times the square of the larger of front and 0)) and ((the neck joint's y minus `S.drop` over `S.unit`) minus y) times `S.unit`. The opening is a vertical cylinder around the neck, wider at the front, that stops `S.drop` below the neck joint, so the neckline follows the base of the neck instead of a fixed height. `hem_y() real` is the pelvis joint's y plus (`S.hem` minus `S.slack`) over `S.unit`: the cut edge of the hem before the drape. `sleeve_end(side int) real` is `S.sleeve` times `arm_len(side)` plus `S.sleeve_slack`, in meters.

`keep(v int) real, int` is how far shell vertex v lies inside the garment, in meters, and which cut is nearest (1 neckline, 2 hem, 3 cuff): the neckline term is `neck_m` of its position; the other term is (its y minus `hem_y()`) times `S.unit` for part 0 (cut 2) and `sleeve_end` of its part minus its `st` for parts 1 and 2 (cut 3); it returns the smaller term and that term's cut (the neckline on a tie).

`cut(P, I)` (same slices as `classify`) builds the work mesh by clipping the shell along the cuts, so every edge of the garment lies exactly on its cut line. Shell vertices with `keep` at least 0 become work vertices in shell order: `wp` and `wa` its position, `wn` its shell normal, `wq` its part, `wt` its `st`, `wh` false, `wg` its position. For every shell triangle with at least one kept corner: walking its corners a, b, c in order, emit each kept corner and, for every edge from one corner to the next whose ends differ in being kept, a cut vertex between them; the resulting polygon (three or four vertices, in that walking order) becomes one or two work triangles fanned from its first vertex, `wr` false. A cut vertex is made once per undirected shell edge (a hash map keyed by the lower shell number times 4294967296 plus the higher) at t equal to the kept end's keep value over (that value minus the dropped end's keep value), measured from the kept end: its position and anchor are the shell positions mixed by t, its anchor normal the shell normals mixed by t and scaled to length one, its `wt` the two `st` values mixed by t, its goal its position; its part is the kept end's part, except that when the dropped end's cut is the neckline its part is 3. Then the boundary: count how many work triangles use each undirected edge (keyed the same way with work vertex numbers); an edge used once is a boundary edge: store it in `wb` in the order its triangle lists it.

`edge_m(v int) real` is how far work vertex v is from the cut it is nearest, in meters: for an arm vertex `sleeve_end` minus its `wt`; for a part 3 vertex 0; for a torso vertex the smaller of (its y minus `hem_y()`) times `S.unit` and `neck_m` of its position. A vertex is in a band when it is an arm vertex with `edge_m` at most `S.cuff`, or a torso vertex below the chest joint's y with `edge_m` at most `S.band`.

`loosen()` moves every work vertex out along its anchor normal by e over `S.unit`, where e is `S.rib_ease` plus (f minus `S.rib_ease`) times the smoothstep of `edge_m` from the edge's band height to the band height plus `S.blouse`; f is `S.ease` for the torso and 0.7 times `S.ease` for the arms; the band height is `S.cuff` for arm vertices, `S.band` for torso vertices below the chest joint's y and 0 for the rest (so the ease also falls to the rib ease near the neckline). Part 3 vertices move by `S.rib_ease` only. Then it marks band vertices held, with goal: a torso vertex its position raised by `S.slack` over `S.unit`, an arm vertex its position moved back toward the shoulder along its segment direction by `S.sleeve_slack` over `S.unit`. Part 3 vertices are held at their own positions. A work triangle is a rib triangle when all three corners are in a band.

`neck_band()` grows the neck band when `S.collar` is above 0. The ring is the longest closed loop of boundary edges from `wb` whose two ends are both part 3, in the edges' own order (each edge's second end is the next edge's first end; follow a table from first end to edge until the loop returns to its start). Five more rings follow it, each with one new vertex per ring vertex: for ring k from 1 to 4 the new vertex is the ring vertex with its x and z offset from the neck joint scaled by 1 minus 0.15 times k over 4, and its y raised by `S.collar` times k over 4 over `S.unit`; ring 5 is ring 4 moved toward the neck joint in x and z by 0.006 over `S.unit` and down by 0.008 over `S.unit`, the folded edge. New vertices are part 3, held at their own position, with the ring vertex's anchor and anchor normal and `wt` 0. Between each pair of consecutive rings, for each pair of loop neighbors a then b (closing from the last back to the first) with a2 and b2 their vertices in the next ring, add the rib work triangles (b, a, a2) and (b, a2, b2), so their winding matches the garment's.

`lips()` turns the hem and cuff edges in. For every boundary edge in `wb` whose two ends are both held arm or torso vertices below the chest joint's y, each end gets one lip vertex (made once per vertex): the vertex moved against its anchor normal by 0.006 over `S.unit`, then for a torso vertex raised by 0.008 over `S.unit`, and for an arm vertex moved toward the shoulder along its segment direction by 0.008 over `S.unit`. A lip vertex copies its source's part, anchor, anchor normal and `wt`, is held, and its goal is the source's goal moved by the same offset. With a then b the edge's ends in `wb` order and la, lb their lip vertices, the edge adds the rib work triangles (b, a, la) and (b, la, lb), so their winding matches the garment's.

`axis_uv(q int, x real, y real, z real) real, real` maps a point to texture coordinates in meters for part q. Torso and neck band (q 0 or 3): with the nearest torso point from `on_part`, u is atan2 of (x minus its x) and (z minus its z) times 0.15 and v is y times `S.unit`. Arm: the arm is mapped as a cylinder bent at the elbow, so the pattern has no seam, flip or stretch there. With A, E and W that arm's shoulder, elbow and wrist joints, d1 the unit direction from A to E and d2 from E to W, L1 is the horizontal offset from the chest joint to A made perpendicular to d1 and scaled to length one, L2 is L1 made perpendicular to d2 and scaled to length one, F1 is the cross product of d1 and L1 and F2 that of d2 and L2. For segment 1: s1 is the dot product of (the point minus A) and d1 (not clamped), r1 the point minus (A plus d1 times s1), and a1 the atan2 of (r1 dot F1) and (r1 dot L1). For segment 2: s2 is the distance from A to E (in shell units) plus the dot product of (the point minus E) and d2, r2 the point minus (E plus d2 times (s2 minus that distance)), and a2 the atan2 of (r2 dot F2) and (r2 dot L2). The blend weight w is the smoothstep from -0.06 to 0.06 of the signed distance in meters of the point from the plane through E whose normal is d1 plus d2 scaled to length one. The angle is a1 plus w times (a2 minus a1 wrapped into minus pi to pi), u is that angle times 0.045 and v is (s1 plus w times (s2 minus s1)) times `S.unit`. The u seam falls at the back of the torso and on the inner side of each arm.

`emit()` turns the work mesh into the results. A work triangle's part is 3 when any corner is part 3, else the part most of its corners have (the lowest on a tie). Each corner is mapped with `axis_uv` of the triangle's part; when the three u values span more than pi times the part's reference radius (0.15 for torso and neck band, 0.045 for arms), add 2 pi times that radius to the u of corners whose u is below 0. Result vertices are made once per work vertex, part and wrapped flag (a table of 8 ints per work vertex: part times 2 plus wrapped, -1 when not made yet) and copy the work vertex's position, anchor, anchor normal, held flag and goal, with the triangle's part and the mapped u and v. The triangle goes to `rib` when its `wr` is true, else to `body`, as u32. Normals: sum the cross products of every result triangle's edges into the work vertex of each corner (via a list of each result vertex's work vertex), scale to length one and copy to every result vertex of that work vertex.

`refine()` splits every result triangle into four through its edge midpoints, `S.fine` times (rounded to an int). A midpoint vertex is made once per undirected edge of result vertices; its position, normal, anchor, anchor normal, uv and goal are the means of the edge's two ends, its part is the first end's part and it is held only when both ends are held. Each triangle stays in its own list (`body` or `rib`). Normals are scaled to length one after.

`tangents()` fills `tan` from `pos`, `nrm`, `uv`, `body` and `rib`. For every triangle with corners 0, 1, 2: e1 and e2 are positions 1 and 2 minus position 0, (du1, dv1) and (du2, dv2) the same for the uvs; skip the triangle when the absolute value of du1 times dv2 minus du2 times dv1 is below 1e-20, else with r one over that value, add (e1 times dv2 minus e2 times dv1) times r to each corner's T sum and (e2 times du1 minus e1 times du2) times r to each corner's V sum. Each vertex's tangent is its T sum minus the normal times their dot product, scaled to length one (when that length is below 1e-12, use the cross product of (0, 1, 0) and the normal, scaled to length one, or (1, 0, 0) when that is also too short), and w is -1 when the dot product of (the cross product of the normal and the tangent) and the V sum is above 0, else 1.

`make(P, I, joints, s Spec) bool` (P: read-only f32 slice of shell positions; I: read-only int slice of shell triangles; joints: read-only f32 slice of 30 numbers in the order of `joint`) stores s in `S` and the joints in `jt`, clears every module array, then runs `classify`, `cut`, `loosen`, `neck_band`, `lips`, `emit`, `refine` and `tangents`. It returns false, leaving the results empty, when joints holds fewer than 30 numbers, P is empty or I's length is not a multiple of 3, or no triangle survives the cut.

`drape(steps int) int` drapes the result with `gfx.cloth` and returns the cloth's number, or -1 when `pos` is empty. It builds one u32 index list of `body` then `rib`, makes the cloth with `cloth::make(pos, 3, 0, count of vertices, that list)`, and sets on `cloth::cloths[c]`: `gravity` 9.81 over `S.unit`, `thickness` 0.003 over `S.unit`, `density` 0.35 times `S.unit` squared, `bend` 0.05 (bend softness: thick knit resists small buckles) and `friction` 0.6. Every vertex gets a body contact with `cloth::skin` at its anchor and anchor normal with gap `S.gap` over `S.unit`. Then for step k from 1 to steps: every held vertex is pinned at its position plus (its goal minus its position) times the smaller of 1 and 2 k over steps, and the cloth steps by 1/60 s with no wind. After the last step every vertex's `pos` becomes its particle's position from `cloth::pos` at `cloth::particle(c, v)` times 3, and normals are rebuilt as in `emit` but summed per position: vertices whose positions are exactly equal share one sum. Then it runs `tangents`.
